title Ten names for one transcript, and the tool that hands you the name
prompt the baton -- BAKERY, core infrastructure, Lindy-first and crux-first
rota lap 4306, row 2 -- Fire, the row that sees; advanced by hand from row 1, read 17 times on this
rota shelf today against row 2's 11. Fire asks what must stop and what must be cut, and its dual
rota seat is the three depths of removal -- so the lap read REDS before anything new and took the
rota oldest thing in it that no hand had claimed: `%620`'s own *Not taken* clause.
think Opened with a scoped cold roster pass: 179 guards, 1,439s, 0 red, 3 gated (all custody), tree
think unmoved, run_verdict=ok. Held still while it ran; drafted into the gitignored .lap/ room,
think which tree_digest passes over, so the drafting could not move the digest the pass was reading.
think Reds first. Of seven OPEN rows, %568 and %456 want Keaton's word on where a cost lands, %457
think and %589 are habits and seams, and %620 carried a clause naming its own unfinished half and
think naming my lane as its owner: whether the roster runner should write its own transcript.
think Measured before believing. Across the pier's eight trees session-output/ holds 165 files
think carrying 42 distinct spellings of this one runner's transcripts, ten of them for the cold pass
think alone. Then the sharp one: `ls grain-copal/session-output/copal-cold-*.txt | tail -1` returns
think the file written at 07:17, not the one at 08:56, because a stamp-named and a pid-named file
think sort against each other by a rule nobody wrote. %620 standing again, on live state, inside the
think room its own repair had moved the file to.
think Named the shape the three firings share. %541 signaled a pass by command line, %549 redirected
think one to a constant /tmp name, %620 globbed a unique one back. In every firing the redirect and
think the naming ran in different shells, so nothing could hold them to one answer.
think So the repair puts both in one shell. `--detach` derives the transcript path from the flags it
think was handed, truncates it, writes a launch header before the child starts, launches the pass
think appending to exactly that path, and prints the path and the child's pid. A lap types no name
think and globs nothing, because the only name is the one it was just handed.
obs Mode names the file rather than a stamp or a pid, and the reason is structural: a second pass in
obs one tree already refuses under run_verdict=run_in_flight, so two live passes of one mode cannot
obs exist to collide. A stamped name is the very thing that made %620 unreadable.
obs Finished is a predicate on CONTENT -- the transcript carries a run_verdict= line, which every
obs exit path emits. An mtime and a process table are both things a second shell can read wrongly,
obs which is the whole family's fault written as a rule.
obs Declined teeing every pass. A foreground pass is read on the terminal as it runs and needs no
obs name at all, so that would touch every invocation by every ship to answer a question only the
obs detached case asks.
obs Proved the truncation from both sides, because it alone is what makes an elder pass's bytes
obs unreadable as this one's. The same launch runs over a runner copy whose header write appends;
obs yesterday's bytes survive it, and the copy is asserted to differ and to actually launch, since a
obs copy that cannot start reads exactly like a repair that worked.
obs The metal proof carried a second reading nobody aimed at: --detach nib_honesty closed
obs tree_moved=no, so writing the transcript into a gitignored room leaves the digest the runner is
obs measuring completely still. That is the block's own claim, proven by the thing it describes.
obs Against myself: the first draft let the parent print `finished_when=run_verdict= line`, and the
obs control leg checking that the parent had NOT run the roster searched for `run_verdict=`. The
obs parent's own help text would have failed that leg. Reworded before it ran.
obs Also against myself: a `cd .lap` persisted between calls and the next command read a path that
obs was not there. The shell's working directory is state a command carries, which is one layer down
obs from this row's own lesson.
obs Against myself, and this is the sharper one: the rebase reported its conflicts and I read that
obs report through `tail -12`, then grepped for markers in the two files the visible lines named.
obs `construction/ITINERARY.md` had conflicted too, above the cut, so a marker block rode into the
obs commit. I asked the files I already suspected rather than asking git which files were unmerged --
obs `git diff --name-only --diff-filter=U` is the question with no window on it. The `conflict_marker`
obs guard caught it on the re-verify, before the push, which is the guard working.
obs And the renumber the derived-spine law predicts: four peers published while this row stood
obs unshared, so `%636` became `%638` and then `%639` on a second rebase. Each move cost one file
obs rename and three lines, because every
obs living citation already spelled the stamp -- which is the whole of rule 4, paid back on the first
obs lap that followed it.
obs One peer repair, named rather than made quietly: the rebase brought in patchouli's live-front
obs block citing a row number one above the ledger's binding for its own stamp, and that number was
obs unshared, so `unshared_citation` reddened for every ship reading this card. Set to the ledger's
obs own `%636`, with the correction written in place beside it.
obs The card sat at its ceiling through all of it: the elder BAKERY block shelved whole and the new
obs one written to fit, five trims to land at 40,958 of 40,960.
